  "The English teen edition quotes the ESV in each chapter's epigraph. ESV wording cannot be carried into Spanish, so every epigraph is in the modern Reina-Valera (1960-register) wording the shipped es corpus quotes, with “el Señor” for LORD, and the “(ESV)” label is dropped (the attribution's ESV clause is dropped with it). Where a shipped *.es.json carries those exact words they were pasted verbatim and the row is `mined` with that file; John 19:30, Psalm 107:29, Matthew 8:20 and Romans 12:21 are not in the corpus and are self_rendered in RV1960 register — a native check against the RV1960 is needed.",
  "Body echoes of the epigraph (fragments, sometimes re-tensed or re-personed for the reader — e.g. Philippians 4:19 “suplir todo lo que te falta”) are self_rendered. Short verbatim phrases the full es edition Una Retrospectiva and the children's edition already use («Al que te pida, dale», «No debáis a nadie nada», «Hasta aquí nos ayudó el Señor», «El Señor proveerá», «Consumado es») are mined from those files so the three Taylor editions agree.",
  "Reader address is tú throughout (matching a-retrospect-children.es); characters in 1850s dialogue address each other as usted-forms where the English implies formality. Outer quotes « », nested “ ”, matching Taylor's es editions. Names and coin terms (media corona, sapeca, Misión al Interior de la China, el doctor Parker, el señor Jones) follow a-retrospect.es. AI-translated in-session; awaiting native review."
